#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build each NixOS host's closure once and put it ON the host, WITHOUT activating it.

PORTED from bitcashier's bin/stage-nixos-closures. The mechanism is unchanged; the transport is
not, and that is the one difference worth reading before anything else here.

  THERE (self-hosted runner, INSIDE the private network): closures were copied to
  `fleet.privateAddress` over 10.0.0.0/8, which is unreachable from the internet, so the ssh path
  itself was a boundary.

  HERE (`ubuntu-latest`, GitHub's shared infrastructure): there is no machine inside 10.20.0.0/24
  that can build anything, so closures are copied over each host's PUBLIC address -- see
  `fleet.publicAddress` in nix/modules/fleet/default.nix. As of writing those are
  mongo-1 2.28.56.140, monitoring-1 128.140.49.167, k3s-worker-1 2.28.47.31, pinned in
  infra/terraform/primary_ips.tf. THEY ARE NOT WRITTEN DOWN IN THIS FILE -- see the note on
  `address_of` for why a roster here would be worse than reading them from the flake.

  WHAT THAT CHANGES ABOUT THE SECURITY ARGUMENT: nothing, and that is the point of the design. The
  key is a forced command (nix/files/nix-stage-endpoint.sh) with `restrict`; the account is not in
  `trusted-users`; every path is SIGNED here and accepted only because the host lists the public
  half. None of those depend on where the client sits. What DOES change is exposure: port 22 is
  open to the world at the Hetzner edge by explicit decision (infra/terraform/firewall.tf), so this
  key is usable from anywhere it leaks to, and it must be a key that exists for nothing else.

WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT. It builds, copies and pins. It never runs `switch-to-configuration`, never
touches the system profile, and never restarts a unit -- so running it can change nothing about a
running machine except how much disk its nix store uses. Activation stays a deliberate act, taken
either by a person or by nix/modules/fleet/auto-apply.nix, which decides LOCALLY, from the
closure's own contents, whether the change would disturb anything.

WHAT IT BUYS. Two things that are worth having separately.

  * ACTIVATION BECOMES INSTANT AND OFFLINE. The expensive, failure-prone half of a deploy is the
    build. With the closure already in the host's store, `switch-to-configuration` is a symlink swap
    and some systemd calls -- and the thing activated is the exact closure CI built and the checks
    ran against, rather than one rebuilt later from a tree somebody hopes matches. On a cx23 with
    2 vCPU that difference is minutes, and on mongo-1 it is minutes during which the machine is
    compiling instead of serving.
  * DRIFT BECOMES VISIBLE AND CHEAP TO CLOSE. "Merged" and "deployed" are different claims on a
    NixOS fleet and nothing on the machine distinguishes them. This makes the second one true by
    default and, when it cannot, says which host and why.

THE GC ROOT IS NOT OPTIONAL. A copied path that nothing refers to is garbage, and
nix/modules/fleet/nix-gc.nix collects nightly -- so a staged closure would silently stop being
staged, and the next merge would re-copy the same gigabyte over the public internet. Each host gets
/var/lib/nixdeploy/staged-system pointing at what was staged for it, replaced on each run, and kept
from the collector by the indirect root /nix/var/nix/gcroots/auto/nixdeploy-staged.

THIS MUST RUN ON x86_64-linux. Evaluating these configurations works anywhere -- a Mac reads them
fine -- but a host closure is x86_64-linux derivations and there is no builder for those on a
developer laptop, so `nix build` there fails after a long download. That is why this is a CI job
rather than something to run before pushing.

  bin/stage-nixos-closures                 # every host this flake declares
  bin/stage-nixos-closures mongo-1         # named configurations only
  SSH_USER=pawel bin/stage-nixos-closures  # as a person, over a normal login

Exit 0 every host has its declared closure staged, 1 at least one could not be staged, 2 nothing
was established at all -- an unreadable flake, or no host named. A host that could not be reached
is a FAILURE, never a quiet success: a closure nobody could copy is not a closure that is there.

NOTE ON TRUST, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE OBVIOUS FIX. A remote daemon accepts an unsigned store
path only from a user in its `trusted-users`, and the copy otherwise fails with "cannot add path ...
because it lacks a signature". The tempting repairs are to add this account to `trusted-users` or to
pass `--no-check-sigs`; both would mean the host accepts whatever this account sends it, which for a
store path is arbitrary code that something will later run. So neither is done.
nix/modules/fleet/default.nix deliberately sets `trusted-users = [ "root" "@wheel" ]` -- CI is NOT in
it -- and every path is SIGNED here with `nix store sign --recursive` before it is copied. What the
host trusts is the key in `fleet.deployStaging.trustedPublicKeys`, not the account that happens to be
connected, which is why losing the ssh key costs a rotation rather than a fleet.
"""
import concurrent.futures
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import time

REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))

# `accept-new` IS A TRUST-ON-FIRST-USE DECISION AND IT IS WEAKER HERE THAN WHERE THIS CAME FROM.
# On a runner inside the private network, first contact could not be intercepted by anything
# outside it. On GitHub's shared infrastructure, over the public internet, a first connection to a
# host whose key this runner has never seen is accepted on faith. THE EXPOSURE IS BOUNDED BY WHAT
# THE KEY CAN DO: an impostor learns the store path of a closure (derivable from this repository by
# anyone who can read it) and receives a signed closure it could have fetched from cache.nixos.org
# anyway. It cannot obtain a shell, a secret, or anything about the real host.
#
# It is still the weakest link in this path, and the fix is known rather than clever: pin the three
# host keys into a known_hosts file generated from terraform and pass `-o UserKnownHostsFile` with
# `StrictHostKeyChecking=yes`. That is worth doing the first time these machines are rebuilt, since
# a rebuild is also when the keys change and when a stale pin would be indistinguishable from an
# attack. Stated as a risk; nothing has gone wrong.
SSH_OPTIONS = ["-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=10",
               "-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"]

# EVERY HOST THIS FLAKE DECLARES IS COVERED, AND THE LIST IS NEVER WRITTEN DOWN HERE.
# `nixosConfigurations` is the enumeration, so a machine minted tomorrow is staged the first time
# this runs after its host file lands -- nobody has to remember to add it, because there is no list
# to add it to. That is the difference between this and a coverage table: a hand-kept roster of
# hosts is a thing that silently stops matching the fleet, which is the failure this whole mechanism
# exists to end.
#
# EMPTY TODAY, AND KEPT ANYWAY. bitcashier's copy of this set holds two entries and both of them
# turned that job RED on the merge that introduced them: a host-agnostic installer image (`base`)
# and a pre-seeded agent snapshot, neither of which describes a machine, so there was nowhere to
# copy them TO -- every real host staged and the run still exited 1. This flake declares three real
# machines and nothing else. The moment somebody adds an installer image, a `*-install` variant or
# a builder-only configuration, IT GOES HERE BY NAME rather than being inferred from a missing
# address: "this configuration describes no machine" and "this host's address could not be
# evaluated" are different claims, and reading the second as the first is how a real host goes
# quietly unstaged.
NOT_A_MACHINE: set = set()


def run(argv, **kwargs):
    result = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=REPO, capture_output=True, text=True, **kwargs)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        detail = (result.stderr or result.stdout).strip().splitlines()
        return None, detail[-1] if detail else f"exit {result.returncode}"
    return result.stdout.strip(), None


def target(address):
    # `nixdeploy` by default: the account whose key is a forced command that can receive a signed
    # closure and pin it, and can do nothing else. SSH_USER overrides it for an admin staging by
    # hand from a Linux box, who reaches a normal shell instead.
    user = os.environ.get("SSH_USER", "nixdeploy").strip()
    return f"{user}@{address}" if user else address


def admin_target(address):
    """Who a PERSON activates as -- never `nixdeploy`.

    The staging account cannot do it, by design and twice over: its key carries
    `command="<endpoint>",restrict`, so anything else a human sends arrives as
    $SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND and is refused, and it has no sudo. So the instruction printed below has
    to name a real login, not the account this tool otherwise talks to.
    """
    user = os.environ.get("SSH_USER", "").strip()
    return f"{user}@{address}" if user else f"<your-login>@{address}"


def endpoint(address, request):
    """Ask the restricted endpoint for something. It reads the request, it does not run it."""
    return run(["ssh"] + SSH_OPTIONS + [target(address), request])


def sign(path):
    """Sign the closure so an UNTRUSTED account's copy is accepted.

    This is what replaces putting the deploy identity in `trusted-users`, and it is the whole
    security argument of this mechanism: a trusted user can insert any path, whereas a signature
    only vouches for paths CI actually built. Absent a key we do not sign and do not pretend to --
    the copy then fails on the host with a missing-signature error, which is the correct and
    legible outcome rather than a silent fallback to an unsigned push.
    """
    key = os.environ.get("NIX_SIGNING_KEY_FILE", "").strip()
    if not key:
        return None
    return run(["nix", "store", "sign", "--key-file", key, "--recursive", path])[1]


def configurations():
    out, error = run(["nix", "eval", "--json", ".#nixosConfigurations",
                      "--apply", "builtins.attrNames"])
    if error:
        return None, error
    return [n for n in json.loads(out) if n not in NOT_A_MACHINE], None


def address_of(name):
    """Where to reach this host, READ FROM THE FLAKE rather than from a table in this file.

    `fleet.publicAddress` is the attribute, and the whole reason it exists (see its option
    documentation) is that CI here is outside the private network. Reading it rather than hardcoding
    the three IPs is not tidiness: an address written in two places is an address that will
    disagree with itself the first time a primary IP is replaced, and the failure that produces is
    an ssh timeout against a machine that is running perfectly -- which reads as "the host is down"
    for as long as it takes somebody to compare this file with terraform.

    AN EMPTY VALUE IS A FAILURE, NOT A SKIP. A host that declares no public address cannot be
    staged from here, and saying so loudly is the point: the alternative is a machine that quietly
    stops tracking main while every run stays green.
    """
    address, error = run(["nix", "eval", "--raw",
                          f".#nixosConfigurations.{name}.config.fleet.publicAddress"])
    if error:
        return None, error
    if not address:
        return None, ("fleet.publicAddress is empty, so there is no address to copy a closure to. "
                      "Set it in this host's file from infra/terraform/primary_ips.tf.")
    return address, None


def stage(name):
    # PER-STEP TIMING, kept from the port. It exists because raising concurrency on the fleet this
    # came from bought almost nothing (105s -> 95s across 29 hosts) instead of the speedup a
    # CPU-bound job would show -- which only makes sense if the SLOWEST host's own SEQUENTIAL chain
    # sets the floor. Here that chain crosses the public internet twice per host instead of a LAN,
    # so the copy step is expected to dominate far more than it did there, and this is what will
    # say so rather than leaving it to be guessed.
    timings = {}

    def timed(label, fn):
        start = time.monotonic()
        result = fn()
        timings[label] = time.monotonic() - start
        return result

    address, error = timed("eval", lambda: address_of(name))
    if error:
        return name, None, "unreachable-by-declaration", error, timings

    # --no-link so this leaves nothing in the working tree; the GC root on the HOST is what keeps
    # the closure alive, and a result symlink here would only keep it alive on a runner that is
    # destroyed minutes later.
    path, error = timed("build", lambda: run(["nix", "build", "--no-link", "--print-out-paths",
                        f".#nixosConfigurations.{name}.config.system.build.toplevel"]))
    if error:
        return name, address, "build failed", error, timings

    running, error = timed("check-current", lambda: endpoint(address, "current"))
    if error:
        return name, address, "unreachable", error, timings

    error = timed("sign", lambda: sign(path))
    if error:
        return name, address, "signing failed", error, timings

    _, error = timed("copy", lambda: run(["nix", "copy", "--to", f"ssh://{target(address)}", path]))
    if error:
        return name, address, "copy failed", error, timings

    # Pinned through the same restricted endpoint, which writes an indirect GC root inside its own
    # directory. Without this the copied closure is unreferenced and the next collection deletes it
    # -- the host would stop being staged with nothing saying so. Writing the pin is ALSO what
    # triggers auto-apply's path unit, so this is the step that makes a merge reach a host.
    _, error = timed("pin", lambda: endpoint(address, f"pin {path}"))
    if error:
        return name, address, "pin failed", error, timings

    return name, address, ("already running" if running == path else "staged"), path, timings


def main():
    wanted = sys.argv[1:]
    names, error = configurations()
    if error:
        print(f"could not read this flake's hosts, so NOTHING was staged: {error}")
        return 2
    if wanted:
        unknown = [n for n in wanted if n not in names]
        if unknown:
            print(f"no such configuration: {', '.join(unknown)}")
            return 2
        names = wanted
    if not names:
        print("this flake declares no host configurations, so nothing was staged")
        return 2

    print(f"staging {len(names)} host closure(s) -- building, copying, pinning; activating nothing")
    if not wanted:
        # Say what the coverage IS, so a reader can check it against the fleet rather than trust it.
        excluded = ", ".join(sorted(NOT_A_MACHINE)) or "nothing"
        print(f"  covering every host this flake declares ({', '.join(sorted(names))}); "
              f"excluded by name: {excluded}")

    # FOUR WORKERS AGAINST THREE HOSTS -- i.e. all of them at once, with the number left where the
    # port had it rather than tuned down to `len(names)`. The work is network-bound and the hosts
    # are independent, so the only cost of a spare worker is a thread that never gets a task, and
    # the only cost of tuning it to today's fleet size is that a fourth host silently serialises.
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool:
        results = sorted(pool.map(stage, names))

    pending, failed = [], []
    for name, address, state, detail, timings in results:
        # WHICH STEP, printed on every line and not just on failures, because the question this
        # exists to answer is "which step is slow on a host that SUCCEEDED".
        breakdown = " ".join(f"{step}={duration:.1f}s" for step, duration in timings.items())
        total = sum(timings.values())
        if state == "staged":
            pending.append((name, address, detail))
            print(f"  STAGED       {name:<22} {address}  total={total:.1f}s  {breakdown}")
        elif state == "already running":
            print(f"  CURRENT      {name:<22} {address}  (nothing to activate)  total={total:.1f}s  {breakdown}")
        else:
            failed.append((name, state, detail))
            print(f"  FAILED       {name:<22} {address or '?'}  {state}: {detail}  total={total:.1f}s  {breakdown}")

    if pending:
        print(f"\n{len(pending)} host(s) have a newer closure staged and NOT activated. Nothing on "
              f"them has changed. To activate one, when you mean to:")
        for name, address, path in pending:
            # BOTH HALVES, AND THE FIRST IS THE ONE THAT IS EASY TO LEAVE OUT.
            # `switch-to-configuration` activates the closure and installs the bootloader, but it
            # does NOT move the system profile. Two things follow, and the second is the expensive
            # one:
            #
            #   * `nixos-rebuild list-generations` and `--rollback` still describe the OLD one, so
            #     the rollback an operator reaches for in an incident takes them somewhere else
            #     entirely -- and this switch records no generation of its own to return to.
            #   * the bootloader installer builds the boot menu FROM that profile. So the running
            #     system is the new one while the DEFAULT BOOT ENTRY still points at the old
            #     generation -- the host is correct until it reboots and then silently is not.
            #
            # This is what `nixos-rebuild switch` does around the same call, and what
            # nix/files/nixos-auto-apply.py's `activate()` does in the same order and for the same
            # reason.
            print(f"  ssh {admin_target(address)} \\")
            print(f"    'sudo nix-env --profile /nix/var/nix/profiles/system --set {path} && \\")
            print(f"     sudo {path}/bin/switch-to-configuration switch'")
        print("\nThat needs no build and no tree on the host -- the closure is already there. See "
              "what it would do first with `dry-activate` in place of `switch`, which is safe to "
              "run on its own and changes nothing (the profile line is not needed to look).")
        print("NOT as `nixdeploy`: that key is a forced command that can only receive and pin a "
              "closure, and the account has no sudo. Use your own login.")
        print("\nOn a host running `fleet.autoApply`, none of this is needed for a change that "
              "disturbs nothing -- it activates itself within about a second of the pin being "
              "written, and what is listed above is what it declined to do unattended.")
    if failed:
        print(f"\n{len(failed)} host(s) could not be staged, so what they would run is not "
              f"prepared and not known.")
        return 1
    if not pending:
        print("\nevery host is already running the closure this tree declares.")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
